Your intuition is correct -- those "bugs" aren't so much bugs as archaisms.

As for the crashes, I've tried compiling Hack 1.0 a couple of times and can 
perhaps give some pointers:

* Older C compilers allowed string constants to be written.  Gcc OTOH will
  put them in the text segment, where any attempt to write them dumps core.
  ISTR a place or two where Hack writes a string constant.  The constant is
  declared as char *foo = "bar"; and changing that to char foo[] = "bar";
  makes it writable again.

* ISTR a couple of places where a string function was called with the wrong
  number of parameters.  But then, if the source code references a proper
  string.h, the compiler will have caught those.

* If all else fails, you can try adding -traditional or -fwritable-strings
  to CFLAGS.

